<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoine de Lamothe Cadillac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1658">1658</a>–<a href="/years/1730">1730</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine de Lamothe Cadillac was a French adventurer and colonial officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. After commanding the post at Michilimackinac, he founded Fort Pontchartrain du Détroit on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> River in 1701, the origin of the city of Detroit. He was later governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, from 1710 to 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoine-de-lamothe-cadillac.jpg" alt="Antoine de Lamothe Cadillac" width="215" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: op unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_de_la_Mothe_Cadillac.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cadillac was born Antoine Laumet on 5 March 1658 at Saint-Nicolas-de-la-Grave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. His father, Jean Laumet, was a lawyer in the Parlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and the boy studied at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> institution. At 25 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and arrived at Port Royal, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, in 1683. On 25 June 1687 he married Marie-Thérèse, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> merchant Denis Guyon. The marriage certificate is the first document to record his new identity, &quot;Antoine de Lamothe, écuyer, sieur de Cadillac&quot;, with titles and arms modelled on those of a baron of Lamothe-Bardigues from villages near his birthplace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed an officer of marine troops in 1690, Cadillac was sent in 1692, as a lieutenant, with the cartographer Jean-Baptiste-Louis Franquelin to chart the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> coast. In 1694 he took command of Fort de Buade at Michilimackinac, where he clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> such as Étienne de Carheil, who accused him of supplying alcohol to Native Americans. After the king ordered trading posts closed in 1696, he proposed a new fort on the strait between Lakes Erie and Huron, which was approved in 1700.</p><p>On 24 July 1701 he led the founding of Fort Pontchartrain du Détroit, with Alphonse de Tonti as second in command. He called the settlement the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. Charged with trafficking in alcohol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, he was imprisoned for a few months in 1704 and cleared in 1705.</p><p>In 1710 the king named him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. He did not obey at once, but sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1711 and persuaded the financier Antoine Crozat to invest there. The Cadillac family reached Fort Louis, now Mobile, in June 1713. In 1715 he and his son Joseph prospected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois-country">Illinois Country</a> and claimed to have found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine, although there is no copper ore there, and he directed the first mining of lead at what is now Mine La Motte, using enslaved workers. After many arguments Crozat withdrew his authority and had him removed from colonial office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cadillac returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he and Joseph were imprisoned in the Bastille for five months. Released in 1718, he was decorated with the Order of Saint Louis, and in 1722 his rights to the concession on the strait were recognised. He became governor and mayor of Castelsarrasin, where he died on 16 October 1730. No authentic portrait of him survives, which historians suggest may reflect a damnatio memoriae started by his many enemies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> grew from his fort, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> company founded in 1902 took his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antoine de la Mothe Cadillac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Nicolas-de-la-Grave, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Castelsarrasin, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.06,1.02&amp;year=1704" aria-label="Saint-Nicolas-de-la-Grave on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4575 604 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antoine de Lamothe Cadillac"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 874h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 874h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.06,1.02&amp;year=1704">What was here in 1704? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
